   "title": "CHINA OWNS 40% OF THE COMPANY RUNNING OUR POWER GRID The Philippine government owns the country's electricity transmission infrastructure, b",
   "url": "https://x.com/tribunephl/status/2115644562534261194",
   "snippet": "CHINA OWNS 40% OF THE COMPANY RUNNING OUR POWER GRID\n\nThe Philippine government owns the country's electricity transmission infrastructure, but the private company operating it, the National Grid Corporation of the Philippines (#NGCP), is 40 percent owned by China's state-owned State Grid Corporation.\n\nThat means the Chinese government holds a substantial stake in the company responsible for transmitting electricity across the Philippines, even as tensions between Manila and Beijing persist in the West Philippine Sea.\n\nNGCP maintains that Filipino engineers operate the grid, and there is no established evidence that China possesses a secret mechanism to shut it down.\n\nBut the ownership arrangement raises a national security question: Should a foreign government with which the Philippines has an ongoing territorial dispute own part of the company operating its electricity transmission network?\n\n#dailytribune",
   "body": "CHINA OWNS 40% OF THE COMPANY RUNNING OUR POWER GRID\n\nThe Philippine government owns the country's electricity transmission infrastructure, but the private company operating it, the National Grid Corporation of the Philippines (#NGCP), is 40 percent owned by China's state-owned State Grid Corporation.\n\nThat means the Chinese government holds a substantial stake in the company responsible for transmitting electricity across the Philippines, even as tensions between Manila and Beijing persist in the West Philippine Sea.\n\nNGCP maintains that Filipino engineers operate the grid, and there is no established evidence that China possesses a secret mechanism to shut it down.\n\nBut the ownership arrangement raises a national security question: Should a foreign government with which the Philippines has an ongoing territorial dispute own part of the company operating its electricity transmission network?\n\n#dailytribune",
